  "question": {
    "text": "If $f(x)$ is a quadratic expression such that $f(1)+f$ (2) $=0$, and $-1$ is a root of $f(x)=0$, then the other root of $f(x)=0$ is",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "0GBj1",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{5}{8}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ygRHe",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{8}{5}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "VSUsV",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{5}{8}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "zTbya",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{8}{5}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "If $a$ and $-1$ are the roots of the polynomial, then we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n& f(x)=x^2+(1-a) x-a . \\\\\n\\therefore \\quad & f(1)=2-2 a \\\\\n\\text { and } & f(2)=6-3 a \\\\\n\\text { As, } & f(1)+f(2)=0 \\\\\n\\Rightarrow & 2-2 a+6-3 a=0 \\Rightarrow a=\\frac{8}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTherefore, the other root is $\\frac{8}{5}$",
    "image": null
  },
